# curl, libcurl4 -- CVE-2026-6276
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# Cookie leak on cross-origin redirect needs curl to both hold cookies (cookie
# engine enabled by -b/-c) and follow a redirect (-L) to another origin. Runtime
# curl callers in the repo: internal/hookinstaller/install.go POSTs to the local
# daemon with curl -ks and no -L/-b/-c; internal/memory/hooks.go does the same
# to DATAWATCH_URL; internal/llm/backends/openwebui/backend.go runs curl -s -N
# against the configured Open WebUI endpoint with a Bearer header, no cookie jar
# and no -L. docker/entrypoint.sh has no curl at all. Dockerfile curl -fsSL
# calls (agent-base rtk, agent-gemini nodesource, parent-full signal-cli) run at
# build time only, with no cookies, so nothing is leaked at runtime. No
# datawatch code path combines cookies with redirects, so the flaw is
# unreachable from datawatch itself. An agent could in theory run curl -b -L by
# hand; that is operator-trusted work, not an exposed path.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm as of this migration; re-check each
# release cycle (BL398 Phase 1's live rescan baseline).
# TRACED: grep of Go sources, hook/entrypoint scripts and image Dockerfiles for
# runtime curl invocations, checking each for redirect-following (-L) and cookie
# options (-b/-c)
CVE-2026-6276

# libldap-2.5-0 -- CVE-2023-2953
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# Null pointer dereference in OpenLDAP ber_memalloc_x, triggered only when an
# LDAP client library parses a malformed response from an LDAP server. The
# earlier note blamed OpenSSH; in Debian bookworm libldap-2.5-0 is actually a
# dependency of libcurl4 and libcurl3-gnutls (curl is built with LDAP/LDAPS
# scheme support), which agent-base installs via curl and git. grep for ldap
# across internal/, cmd/, scripts/ and the agent-base, agent-gemini, agent-aider
# and parent-full Dockerfiles finds no LDAP use, no ldap URL, and no slapd or
# ldap-utils package. The only way to reach the code is curl being handed an
# ldap:// or ldaps:// URL pointing at a hostile server; every curl call in the
# repo targets http(s) endpoints (local daemon, operator-configured LLM URL,
# github/nodesource at build time). The library cannot be removed without
# removing curl and git, so no removal is recommended.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm as of this migration; re-check each
# release cycle (BL398 Phase 1's live rescan baseline).
# TRACED: grep of Go sources, scripts and Dockerfiles for ldap usage; apt
# dependency reasoning for how libldap-2.5-0 enters the images
CVE-2023-2953

# ncurses (libncursesw6, libtinfo6, ncurses-base) -- CVE-2025-69720
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# CVE-2025-69720 is a stack buffer overflow in analyze_string() in
# progs/infocmp.c, i.e. in the infocmp command-line tool, not in the terminal
# escape-sequence parser the earlier wording described (ncurses before
# 6.5-20251213). Only the infocmp binary reaches that function, and infocmp
# ships in ncurses-bin, which is NOT installed in agent-base (dpkg-query finds
# no ncurses-bin; which infocmp tic toe tput clear finds nothing). The flagged
# packages are only libraries: libtinfo6 is linked by bash, tmux, less,
# util-linux, libreadline8 and procps, libncursesw6 by procps and
# pinentry-curses, ncurses-base is terminfo data. None of them contains
# analyze_string. No Dockerfile or entrypoint and no Go source under internal/
# or cmd/ invokes infocmp, and the container runs as uid 10001. Unreachable; the
# libraries are required by tmux, bash and less.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm as of this migration; re-check each
# release cycle (BL398 Phase 1's live rescan baseline).
# TRACED: docker run --network none on the built agent-base image: dpkg-query,
# apt-cache rdepends --installed, which infocmp/tic/tput; grep of Dockerfiles
# and internal/ cmd/ Go sources; CVE description read
CVE-2025-69720

# perl, perl-base, perl-modules-5.36, libperl5.36 -- CVE-2026-9538
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# CVE-2026-9538 is a memory-exhaustion flaw in Perl's Archive::Tar before 3.10:
# the entry size is read from the 12-byte tar header with no upper bound, so a
# crafted archive makes Perl allocate a huge buffer (denial of service). It only
# triggers when a Perl program calls Archive::Tar on an untrusted archive. In
# agent-base, perl is needed by git (git Depends: perl, liberror-perl, so it
# cannot be dropped while git stays) and by debconf and adduser; git 2.39.5's
# Perl scripts do not use Archive::Tar (grep of /usr/lib/git-core finds none)
# and git archive and git clone are C code. The only Archive::Tar callers are
# the perl-provided ptar, ptardiff, ptargrep and instmodsh utilities in
# /usr/bin, which nothing runs. No datawatch Go source or image Dockerfile or
# entrypoint executes perl or those tools, and tar is extracted with GNU tar.
# The path is unreachable.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm. GH#163 (2026-10-07) already removed
# CVE-2026-42496/42497/48962/8376 from this block once a fix shipped for those
# four -- same removal process applies here if/when one ships.
# TRACED: docker run --network none on the built agent-base image: dpkg Depends
# of git, apt-cache rdepends --installed perl, grep for Archive::Tar in
# /usr/lib/git-core, dpkg -S /usr/bin/ptar; grep of Go sources and Dockerfiles;
# CVE description read
CVE-2026-9538

# bsdutils, libblkid1, libmount1, libsmartcols1, libuuid1, mount, util-linux, util-linux-extra -- CVE-2026-53615
# severity: high; images: agent-base
# CVE-2026-53615 is in libblkid's parse_dos_extended(): a 32-bit sum for the
# extended-partition start sector and missing bounds checks let a crafted DOS
# extended-partition table be misreported. It needs libblkid to probe a block
# device or disk image holding that table. In agent-base the library is pulled
# in by util-linux, mount and libmount1 (apt-cache rdepends); the blkid, lsblk,
# mount, umount, findmnt and wipefs binaries exist, but ldd shows neither tmux
# nor git links libblkid or libmount. No Dockerfile or entrypoint calls them,
# and no Go source under internal/ or cmd/ runs blkid, lsblk, mount or fdisk
# (internal/observer/disk_linux.go only parses /proc mount information and calls
# statfs). The container runs as uid 10001 with no /dev/sd*, nvme or loop
# devices, so nothing can probe a crafted partition table. Unreachable;
# util-linux is essential and cannot be removed.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm:
# security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-53615 (checked 2026-10-21) lists
# bookworm 2.38.1-5+deb12u3 as vulnerable with no fixed version (Debian bug
# 1140197); fixed only in trixie 2.41.5-0+deb13u1. Review by 2026-11-20 and
# remove once Debian ships a bookworm util-linux update (see
# docs/plans/2026-10-21-cve-2026-53615-agent-base.md).
# TRACED: docker run --network none on the built agent-base image: dpkg-query,
# apt-cache rdepends --installed for libblkid1/libmount1/mount, ldd of tmux and
# git, which blkid lsblk mount; grep of Go sources and Dockerfiles; CVE
# description read
CVE-2026-53615

# libcups2 -- CVE-2026-55453
# severity: high; images: parent-full
# CVE-2026-55453 is in the CUPS scheduler/backend: quote_string() in
# backend/ipp.c keeps CR/LF in a printer-supplied marker message, so a hostile
# IPP printer can inject a PPD control line that cupsd saves, which only matters
# when cupsd later runs a print job on a Foomatic-backed queue. In parent-full
# the package is only the libcups2 client library, pulled in by
# Dockerfile.parent-full line 23 (install_packages openjdk-17-jre-headless) on
# top of agent-base, where dpkg shows no libcups2, no cups, cups-daemon,
# cups-browsed or cups-filters. So there is no scheduler, no backend/ipp binary,
# no queue and no PPD store in the image; the vulnerable function lives in the
# cups daemon backend, not in libcups2. The only Java program run is signal-cli
# (internal/signal/javafix.go runs signal-cli --version and the daemon); it is
# headless and never calls javax.print. grep of cmd/, internal/, docker/ and
# scripts/ finds no lp, lpr, lpstat, cupsd, ipp:// or .ppd use. Unreachable;
# libcups2 is a hard Depends of openjdk-17-jre-headless so it cannot be removed
# separately.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm:
# security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-55453 (checked 2026-10-21) lists
# bookworm 2.4.2-3+deb12u9 as vulnerable with no fixed version (Debian bug
# 1150560); fixed only in sid 2.4.20-1. Review by 2026-11-20 and remove once
# Debian ships a bookworm cups update (see
# docs/plans/2026-10-21-trivy-aider-parent-full.md).
# TRACED: grep of Dockerfiles, docker/entrypoint.sh and Go sources for
# cups/lp/lpr/ipp; dpkg and apt-cache inspection of the built agent-base image
# (docker run --network none); parent-full Dockerfile and internal/signal read;
# CVE description read
CVE-2026-55453

# git-lfs -- CVE-2025-26625
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# CVE-2025-26625: git-lfs can be made to write or read outside the repo through
# a crafted symlink/pointer when it checks out LFS objects from a hostile
# repository or LFS server. Dockerfile.agent-base line 146 installs git-lfs, so
# all four images carry it. Running the built image shows /etc/gitconfig already
# contains filter.lfs.clean, filter.lfs.smudge, filter.lfs.process and
# filter.lfs.required=true, so git invokes git-lfs automatically on any checkout
# of a repo whose .gitattributes marks files filter=lfs, without anyone typing
# git lfs. internal/agents/worker_clone.go runs git clone (optionally --branch)
# and git pull --ff-only into the worker workspace, and the agent sessions run
# git themselves, so the vulnerable code path is reachable. No datawatch Go
# source, script or entrypoint calls git lfs explicitly (grep for lfs in
# cmd/internal/scripts/docker found only the Dockerfile install line). Exposure
# is limited: the clone URL is the operator-configured project remote
# (resp.Git.URL), so exploitation needs an operator to point a project at an
# attacker-controlled LFS-enabled remote. Reachable but low likelihood; keep the
# suppression until Debian ships a bookworm fix.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm as of this migration; re-check each
# release cycle (BL398 Phase 1's live rescan baseline).
# TRACED: grep of Dockerfiles, entrypoint and Go sources for lfs and git
# clone/pull; docker run --network none on the built agent-base image to read
# /etc/gitconfig and git-lfs dpkg contents; internal/agents/worker_clone.go
# read; CVE description read
CVE-2025-26625

# python3.11, libpython3.11-* -- CVE-2026-7210
# severity: critical; images: agent-aider, agent-gemini
# CVE-2026-7210 is a use-after-free in Python 3.11's expat bindings
# (xml.parsers.expat, used by xml.etree.ElementTree) triggered by crafted XML.
# agent-gemini: python3.11 is not installed by its Dockerfile; apt-cache
# rdepends lists nodejs and rsync as the dependents, there is no Python
# application in the image (gemini is a node program), and a grep finds no
# caller, so there it is dormant. agent-aider: Dockerfile.agent-aider installs
# python3 and runs aider from a pipx venv whose interpreter is /usr/bin/python3
# (3.11.2), so the stdlib expat module is live. Grep of the venv shows
# ElementTree used by botocore (botocore/serialize.py and compat.py parse AWS
# service XML responses), by litellm (get_blog_posts.py calls ET.fromstring on
# an RSS feed fetched over httpx; prompt_templates/factory.py parse_xml_params
# calls ET.fromstring on model-produced function-call XML) and by networkx gexf.
# So untrusted XML from an LLM provider, AWS endpoint or remote feed can reach
# the vulnerable parser in agent-aider. Reachable in agent-aider, conditional on
# a hostile or compromised provider response; the datawatch Go service itself
# never parses XML in Python.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm as of this migration; re-check each
# release cycle (BL398 Phase 1's live rescan baseline).
# TRACED: docker run --network none on the built agent-gemini and agent-aider
# images: dpkg -l, apt-cache rdepends --installed python3.11-minimal, grep of
# the aider pipx venv site-packages for xml.etree/xml.dom/pyexpat imports, read
# of the litellm and botocore call sites; Dockerfile.agent-gemini/aider read
CVE-2026-7210

# python3.11, libpython3.11-* -- CVE-2025-69534
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-gemini
# Python 3.11 installed in agent images for aider and other Python-based tools;
# the datawatch service itself is Go. CVE-2026-7210:
# xml.parsers.expat/ElementTree use-after-free via crafted XML -- agent tooling
# does not expose Python XML parsers to untrusted input. CVE-2025-69534:
