# libpython3.11-minimal -- CVE-2026-19445
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-gemini
# Use-after-free of a server-side SSLContext when sni_callback switches contexts
# mid-handshake (3.11.2-6+deb12u9). Grepped every installed Python package
# (stdlib + both images' full site-packages, including agent-aider's pipx venv)
# for sni_callback: zero hits anywhere except the stdlib's own ssl.py attribute
# definition. aiohttp.worker does build a server-side SSLContext, but the only
# aiohttp importers found in agent-aider are Google auth's async client
# transport wrappers, not a server. The vulnerable code path is confirmed
# unreachable.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm. Independently traced and confirmed
# unreachable this session -- re-check for a fix each cycle; re-trace only if
# either tool's dependency tree changes meaningfully.
# TRACED: grep of installed site-packages in networkless container (docker run
# --entrypoint /bin/sh --network none)
CVE-2026-19445

# libexpat1 -- CVE-2026-77214
# severity: high; images: agent-aider, agent-base, agent-gemini, parent-full
# Heap buffer over-read in XML_ParseBuffer, triggered by
# malformed/attacker-controlled XML. Scanned every file in the image for a
# dynamic link to libexpat1: only /usr/lib/git-core/git-http-push links it. That
# binary is git's legacy dumb-HTTP/WebDAV push path, superseded by the
# smart-HTTP protocol git push has used by default since ~2010; it is never
# invoked by any datawatch code path, the image's own git usage, or a normal
# `git push` to a modern remote (GitHub/GitLab/etc. all serve smart HTTP). Even
# in the dumb-HTTP case the exposure is on push, not fetch, requiring the
# operator's own configured remote to be malicious -- not an attacker-controlled
# input.
# Found live by image-refresh.yaml's blocking scan (new since the BL398
# migration baseline -- not in the original 70). No fix available in Debian
# bookworm. Independently traced and confirmed unreachable this session.
# TRACED: ldd scan of every executable in a networkless container (docker run
# --entrypoint /bin/sh --network none) to find every real linker to libexpat1,
# then traced each caller's actual invocation path
CVE-2026-77214

# openjdk-17-jre-headless -- CVE-2026-91403
# severity: critical; images: parent-full
# The JRE is installed in parent-full for one reason only: to run signal-cli
# (see the comment above the install line in Dockerfile.parent-full). A source
# trace of the repository shows java is started only by internal/signal
# (signalcli.go, javafix.go). CVE-2026-91403 distrusts eMudhra intermediate CA
# certificates; it affects signal-cli only if the Signal service chain used an
# eMudhra intermediate, and Signal pins its own trust root, so it does not.
# CVE-2026-91415 is a font-rendering hardening fix (Oracle CPU 2026-10); it
# needs AWT text rendering, and signal-cli is a headless messaging client that
# never renders text. Both paths are unreachable from datawatch code.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm (status affected, no fixed version). Found
# live by the Release workflow blocking parent-full scan on the 9.5.93 tag.
# Moved from a hand edit of .trivyignore into this registry on 2026-10-24.
# Re-check for a fix each cycle; re-trace only if signal-cli use of the JRE
# changes.
# TRACED: source grep of every java invocation in the repository (source trace;
# not a container linker scan)
CVE-2026-91403

