# libbrotli1 -- CVE-2026-111497
# severity: critical; images: agent-base
# A buffer overflow in the Brotli decoder, reachable only when something decodes
# attacker-supplied Brotli data. ldd scan of every executable and shared library
# in the agent-base image found exactly these linkers to libbrotlidec:
# /usr/bin/curl, libcurl and libcurl-gnutls, and git's HTTP helpers
# (git-remote-http, git-http-fetch, git-http-push, git-imap-send). Nothing else
# in the image, and no datawatch code, decodes Brotli. The data reaches the
# decoder only if an HTTP server the tool talks to answers with Content-Encoding
# br: curl asks for it only with --compressed (no datawatch script uses it) and
# git's HTTP transport accepts whatever libcurl supports, so the exposure is a
# git remote that the operator configured and that is controlled by an attacker,
# over a TLS connection the attacker cannot alter in transit. The remotes agents
# use are the operator's own (GitHub and local mirrors). Not exploitable without
# a hostile remote.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm (status affected). Found live by the
# Release workflow blocking agent-base scan on the 9.5.111 tag. Moved from a
# hand edit of .trivyignore into this registry on 2026-10-24. Re-check for a fix
# each cycle; re-trace only if a new Brotli-linked tool is added to the image.
# TRACED: ldd scan in a networkless container of the built agent-base image
# (docker run --entrypoint /bin/sh --network none)
CVE-2026-111497

# openjdk-17-jre-headless -- CVE-2026-114820
# severity: high; images: parent-full
# The JRE is installed in parent-full only to run signal-cli
# (Dockerfile.parent-full). A source trace shows java is started only by
# internal/signal (signalcli.go, javafix.go). CVE-2026-114820 is an Oracle Java
# SE advisory (2026-10-24) in the JSSE component, CVSS 3.1 9.1, exploitable over
# the network through APIs that process TLS data. signal-cli uses JSSE as a TLS
# client to the Signal service; datawatch starts it with a local config
# directory and does not expose a TLS listener from the JVM, and the container
# publishes no JVM port. An attacker would therefore have to be a hostile TLS
# peer of signal-cli (the Signal service endpoints or a man in the middle of
# that connection), which signal-cli's pinned trust store makes difficult, but
# the client-side code path is real and the vulnerable APIs were not traced
# further, so reachability is recorded as unknown rather than no. Messaging over
# Signal is an optional feature; the image is not used without it.
# No fix available in Debian bookworm:
# security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-114820 (checked 2026-10-30) lists
# bookworm security 17.0.21+9-1~deb12u1 as vulnerable; fixed only in sid
# (17.0.21.0.3+1-1). Found live by the v9.39.0 Release workflow blocking
# parent-full scan. Short expiry: re-check every few days and consume the Debian
# update as soon as it ships (security patches are consumed when available).
# TRACED: source grep of every java invocation in the repository (source trace;
# not a container linker scan); CVE description read on NVD and the Debian
# security tracker, 2026-10-30
CVE-2026-114820
